@page
@model BeeMemoryBank.Web.Pages.Admin.BlindNodesModel

@section About {
    <p>
        A <strong>blind node</strong> is a machine that keeps an encrypted copy of this network and of its backups, and cannot read any of it: it never holds the
        key. It is an off-site safe for your data. If every full node were lost, the blind node still holds what a restore needs; only you can open it, with your master password.
    </p>
    <p>
        Use the actions below to invite a copy or add one. Open <em>Details</em> for how invitations, pair codes and hubs work and what a blind node can see.
    </p>
    <details id="blind-node-details">
        <summary><strong>Details</strong></summary>
        <div class="admin-notes" style="margin-top:12px;">
            <p>
                To let a friend keep a copy on their own device (the <strong>Blind copy</strong> app on a phone, a PC or a Mac), make an <strong>invitation</strong> below:
                you get one line of text and a QR code that work once, for a few minutes. Send them to your friend by a channel you trust. Their app asks to join and shows a
                short <strong>match code</strong>; you compare it with the one shown here and confirm with your master password. Until you confirm, no vault content and no usable key of your memory bank is sent to their device.
                Asking to join already sends this node their device id, public key, name, platform, version and their own backup key; the node keeps that backup key encrypted until you decide. You
                can withdraw an invitation that has not been used.
            </p>
            <p>
                The older way is the <strong>pair code</strong>, and it is the only way for a blind node on a server (Docker or a service): its own console shows the code, and you paste it in
                the box below. This PC checks the node's key, checks that every other device already sees this PC as a superadmin and runs a current version, and then sends the
                node its first copy. A phone in &ldquo;Blind copy&rdquo; mode can still be added the older way too, with the code it shows (the card &ldquo;Add an Android blind copy&rdquo;). Each blind copy's
                status and alarms are listed at the end of the page, where you can also reseed a blind node (send it a fresh copy) or disconnect it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The page also lists the <em>hubs</em> for blind copies: the full nodes a blind copy (on a PC, a Mac or a phone) is allowed to call. You set a hub up under
                <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Trusted Nodes</a>, with &ldquo;Let blind copies call this node&rdquo;.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted text-sm" id="blind-sees">
                <strong>What it can see.</strong> With metadata encryption on &mdash; the default for a network set up with 2.6.2 or later &mdash; a blind node sees
                no titles, no paths, no folder, tag or file names and no shape of your tree: only that something changed (how many changes, how big, when,
                and which of your devices made them), plus the names and addresses of your devices. On an older network where metadata encryption has not
                been switched on yet (<a href="/Admin/MetadataEncryption">Metadata Encryption</a>), a blind node can still see titles, paths, tags and file names, but never the text
                of a note or what is inside a file.
            </p>
        </div>
    </details>
}

@if (Model.WarningMessage != null)
{
    <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-bottom:12px;">@Model.WarningMessage</sl-alert>
}
@* Invite a friend's device (release 2.7). One-time invitation: not encrypted, said so. Until the owner confirms below, no vault content and no usable key is sent to the device; asking to join already sends this node the device's own data (id, public key, name, platform, version, its backup key), and the node keeps that key encrypted until the decision. *@
<h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="envelope-plus"></sl-icon> Invite a blind copy</h3>
<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:24px;" id="invite">
    <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
        Let a friend keep an encrypted copy of your network on their device. You send them one line (or a QR code); their app asks to join,
        shows a short code, and you confirm that code here with your master password. Until you confirm, no vault content and no usable key of your memory bank is sent to their device.
        Their app does send this node its own device id, public key, name, platform, version and its own backup key when it asks to join; this node keeps that backup key encrypted until you decide.
        This is a <strong>one-time invitation</strong>, not an encrypted message: whoever has it while it is valid can ask to join,
        which is why you confirm against the code your friend reads out to you.
    </p>

    @if (Model.InviteError is { } inviteError)
    {
        <sl-alert variant="danger" open role="alert" id="invite-error" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
            <sl-icon slot="icon" name="exclamation-octagon"></sl-icon>
            @inviteError
        </sl-alert>
    }

    @if (Model.Issued is { } issued)
    {
        <div id="invite-issued" data-expires="@issued.ExpiresAt.ToString("o")" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
            <sl-alert variant="success" open style="margin-bottom:10px;">
                The invitation is ready. It works for one device, once, until <strong>@issued.ExpiresAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("t")</strong>
                (<span id="invite-countdown">@((issued.ExpiresAt - DateTime.UtcNow).TotalMinutes.ToString("0")) min</span> left).
            </sl-alert>
            <textarea id="invite-text" rows="5" readonly style="width:100%;font-family:monospace;font-size:0.8rem;">@issued.Text</textarea>
            <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:8px;align-items:center;">
                <sl-button id="invite-copy" size="small" variant="primary"><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="clipboard"></sl-icon> Copy</sl-button>
                <span id="invite-copied" class="text-muted text-sm" hidden>Copied.</span>
            </div>
            @if (Model.IssuedQr is { } qr)
            {
                <div style="margin-top:12px;">
                    <img src="@qr" alt="QR code of the invitation" style="width:260px;height:260px;image-rendering:pixelated;background:#fff;padding:8px;" />
                    <div class="text-muted text-sm">The same invitation as a QR code, for a phone camera.</div>
                </div>
            }
            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(issued.Notice))
            {
                <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:10px 0 0;">@issued.Notice</p>
            }
            <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:10px 0 0;">
                It is shown here once. Send it to your friend by a channel you trust (not a public one), and ask them to read you the code their app shows.
            </p>
        </div>
    }

    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Invite" data-busy="long" data-busy-title="Checking this node's address and making the invitation">
        <div class="admin-fields">
            <sl-input name="label" label="Who is it for? (your own note)" maxlength="64" value="@Model.InviteLabel" placeholder="For example: Anna's phone"></sl-input>
            <sl-input name="sourceName" label="Name shown to your friend" maxlength="64" value="@Model.InviteSourceName" help-text="Your friend's app says: a copy of this person's Bee Memory Bank."></sl-input>
        </div>
        <div class="admin-fields" style="margin-top:12px;">
            <sl-input class="admin-field-wide" name="address" label="Address your friend's device will call" value="@Model.InviteAddress"
                      help-text="This node's public https address. It is checked now: it must lead back to this very node."></sl-input>
            <div>
                <label for="invite-lifetime" class="text-sm" style="display:block;margin-bottom:4px;">Valid for</label>
                <select id="invite-lifetime" name="lifetime">
                    @foreach (var minutes in Model.InviteDefaults?.Lifetimes ?? [15, 30, 60])
                    {
                        <option value="@minutes" selected="@(minutes == Model.InviteLifetime)">@minutes minutes</option>
                    }
                </select>
            </div>
        </div>
        <sl-button type="submit" variant="primary" size="small" style="margin-top:12px;">
            <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="envelope-plus"></sl-icon> Make invitation
        </sl-button>
    </form>

    @foreach (var open in Model.OpenInvites)
    {
        <div class="article-meta" style="margin-top:10px;">
            <span>Open invitation@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(open.Label) ? "" : " for " + open.Label)</span>
            <span>valid until @open.ExpiresAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("t")</span>
            <form method="post" asp-page-handler="WithdrawInvite" data-busy="short" style="display:inline;">
                <input type="hidden" name="inviteId" value="@open.InviteId" />
                <sl-button type="submit" size="small" variant="default">Withdraw</sl-button>
            </form>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@if (Model.WaitingInvites.Any())
{
    <h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="hourglass-split"></sl-icon> Waiting for your confirmation</h3>
    @foreach (var waiting in Model.WaitingInvites)
    {
        <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:12px;" id="waiting-@waiting.InviteId">
            <div class="article-meta">
                <span><strong>@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(waiting.Label) ? waiting.DeviceName : waiting.Label)</strong></span>
                @if (waiting.Kind == "server")
                {
                    <span class="invite-kind-server"><sl-icon name="hdd-network"></sl-icon> Invited (server)</span>
                }
                <span>@(waiting.Kind == "server" ? "Server node" : "Device"): @waiting.DeviceName</span>
                <span>@(waiting.Platform is { Length: > 0 } platform ? platform : "platform unknown")@(waiting.AppVersion is { Length: > 0 } version ? " " + version : "")</span>
                <span>From @(waiting.SeenFrom ?? "an unknown address")</span>
                <span>Asked @(waiting.RedeemedAt?.ToLocalTime().ToString("g") ?? "")</span>
                @if (waiting.ConfirmDeadline is { } deadline)
                {
                    <span>Answer by @deadline.ToLocalTime().ToString("g")</span>
                }
            </div>
            <div style="margin:12px 0;">
                <div class="text-muted text-sm">Ask your friend to read you the code @(waiting.Kind == "server" ? "that bmb blind join printed on their server" : "their app shows"). Confirm only if it is exactly this one:</div>
                <div class="invite-match-code" style="font-family:monospace;font-size:2rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.15em;">@waiting.MatchCode</div>
            </div>
            <form method="post" asp-page-handler="ConfirmInvite" data-busy="long" data-busy-title="Adding the blind copy" style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap;">
                <input type="hidden" name="inviteId" value="@waiting.InviteId" />
                <sl-input name="password" label="Your master password" type="password" required password-toggle autocomplete="off" style="min-width:260px;"></sl-input>
                <sl-button type="submit" variant="primary" size="small"><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="check2-circle"></sl-icon> The code matches: confirm</sl-button>
                @if (waiting.Kind == "server")
                {
                    <div class="text-muted text-sm" style="flex-basis:100%;">Confirming also sends this server your memory bank's encrypted copy, as pasting its pair code on this page would. You will manage it (reseed, anchors, recovery).</div>
                }
            </form>
            <form method="post" asp-page-handler="RefuseInvite" data-busy="short" style="margin-top:8px;">
                <input type="hidden" name="inviteId" value="@waiting.InviteId" />
                <sl-button type="submit" size="small" variant="danger" outline><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="x-circle"></sl-icon> Refuse: this is not my friend</sl-button>
            </form>
        </div>
    }
}
@if (Model.FinishingInvites.Any())
{
    <div class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:0 0 16px 0;" id="invites-finishing">
        Being added:
        @foreach (var finishing in Model.FinishingInvites)
        {
            <span style="margin-right:12px;">@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(finishing.Label) ? finishing.DeviceName ?? "a device" : finishing.Label)@(finishing.Kind == "server" ? " (server)" : "")</span>
        }
        This node finishes it within a minute, or undoes it and says so here.
    </div>
}
@if (Model.DecidedInvites.Any())
{
    <div class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin:-12px 0 24px 0;">
        Lately:
        @foreach (var decided in Model.DecidedInvites)
        {
            <span style="margin-right:12px;">@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(decided.Label) ? decided.DeviceName ?? "invitation" : decided.Label)@(decided.Kind == "server" ? " (server)" : "") (@decided.State)</span>
        }
    </div>
}

<h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="plus-circle"></sl-icon> Add a blind node</h3>
<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:24px;" id="add-by-pair-code">
    <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
        Paste the pair code from the blind node's console. This PC checks the node's key, checks that
        every other device already sees this PC as superadmin and runs a current version, then sends
        the node its first copy.
    </p>
    <form data-busy="long" data-busy-title="Adding the blind node" method="post" asp-page-handler="Add">
        <textarea name="code" rows="3" placeholder="BMBBLIND1...." required style="width:100%;font-family:monospace;">@Model.Code</textarea>
        <sl-button type="submit" variant="primary" size="small" style="margin-top:8px;">
            <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="link-45deg"></sl-icon> Add
        </sl-button>
    </form>
</div>

@* The page "Add an Android blind copy" used to be a page of its own, reached by a link from here; its result (the code that tells the phone where to call) shows in this card. *@
<h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="phone"></sl-icon> Add an Android blind copy</h3>
<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:24px;" id="android-blind-copy">
    <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
        A phone in "Blind copy" mode keeps an encrypted copy of this network and makes backups, but never
        holds the master key. It does not listen for connections: it calls a blind node or a hub (a full node
        set up for it), which must already be in this network.
    </p>

    @if (Model.PhoneError != null)
    {
        <sl-alert variant="danger" open id="phone-error" role="alert" style="margin-bottom:12px;">@Model.PhoneError</sl-alert>
    }

    @if (Model.Paired != null)
    {
        <div id="call-code">
            <p style="margin-bottom:12px;">
                <strong>@Model.Paired.DisplayName</strong> is in the network. On the phone, scan this code or paste
                its text: it tells the phone where to call and which certificate to trust.
            </p>
            <img src="@Model.CallCodeQr" alt="Where-to-call code" style="width:256px;height:256px;image-rendering:pixelated;" />
            <textarea readonly rows="3" style="width:100%;font-family:monospace;margin-top:8px;">@Model.Paired.CallCode</textarea>
        </div>
    }
    else if (Model.Listeners == null)
    {
        <sl-alert variant="warning" open>Could not load the nodes a phone can call. Check the API connection.</sl-alert>
    }
    else if (Model.Listeners.Count == 0)
    {
        <sl-alert variant="warning" open>
            This network has no node a phone can call. Add a blind node first, or set up a hub: under <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Trusted Nodes</a>,
            "Let blind copies call this node" on the server a phone should call. This PC alone cannot serve a phone.
        </sl-alert>
    }
    else
    {
        <form data-busy="long" data-busy-title="Pairing the Android blind copy" method="post" asp-page-handler="PairPhone">
            <label for="phone-code">Code shown by the phone</label>
            <textarea id="phone-code" name="code" rows="3" placeholder="bmb-blind-phone:?..." required style="width:100%;font-family:monospace;"></textarea>
            <label for="phone-listener" style="display:block;margin-top:12px;">The phone will call</label>
            <select id="phone-listener" name="listenerId" required>
                @foreach (var l in Model.Listeners)
                {
                    <option value="@l.NodeId">@l.DisplayName (@(l.IsBlind ? "blind node" : "hub"), @l.TrustLabel, @l.Address)</option>
                }
            </select>
            <div style="margin-top:12px;">
                <sl-button type="submit" variant="primary" size="small">
                    <sl-icon slot="prefix" name="link-45deg"></sl-icon> Add
                </sl-button>
            </div>
        </form>
    }
</div>

<h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="broadcast-pin"></sl-icon> Hubs for blind copies</h3>
<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:24px;" id="hubs">
    <p class="text-muted text-sm" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
        A blind copy (Windows, macOS, Android) calls a node; a full node you set up for it is a hub. Set one up under Admin,
        <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Trusted Nodes</a>, "Let blind copies call this node". A hub with a normal certificate is trusted by the system's list of
        authorities; one with a pinned certificate only by its own key.
    </p>
    @if (Model.Hubs == null)
    {
        <p class="text-muted">Could not load the hubs. Check the API connection.</p>
    }
    else if (Model.Hubs.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="text-muted" style="margin:0;">No hubs yet. <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Set one up in Trusted Nodes</a>.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        @foreach (var hub in Model.Hubs)
        {
            <div class="article-meta" style="margin-bottom:6px;">
                <span><strong>@hub.DisplayName</strong></span>
                <span>@hub.Address</span>
                <sl-badge variant="primary" pill>@hub.TrustLabel</sl-badge>
            </div>
        }
        <p class="text-sm" style="margin:8px 0 0;">Change or turn off a hub in <a href="/Admin/TrustedNodes">Trusted Nodes</a>. A lost blind copy is revoked there too.</p>
    }
</div>

<h3 style="margin-bottom:12px;"><sl-icon name="hdd-network"></sl-icon> Connected blind nodes</h3>
@if (Model.Nodes == null)
{
    <sl-alert variant="warning" open>Could not load blind nodes. Check the API connection.</sl-alert>
}
else if (Model.Nodes.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="text-muted">No blind nodes yet.</p>
}
else
{
    @foreach (var node in Model.Nodes)
    {
        <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
            <div class="article-meta">
                <span><strong>@node.DisplayName</strong></span>
                <span class="article-id-badge">@node.NodeId</span>
                <span>@(node.Address ?? "no address")</span>
                <span>Protocol: @(node.Protocol?.ToString() ?? "unknown")</span>
                <span>Last contact: @(node.LastContact?.ToLocalTime().ToString("g") ?? "never")@(node.Contact?.LastContactDirection is { } direction ? " (" + BeeMemoryBank.Web.Pages.Admin.AdminText.ContactDirectionWords(direction) + ")" : "")</span>
                @if (node.Contact?.LastPushAt is { } pushedAt)
                {
                    <span>Changes delivered: @pushedAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("g")</span>
                }
                @if (node.Contact is { State: "unknown" } && node.Address is not null)
                {
                    <span>Not checked since this node started</span>
                }
                @if (node.AdoptedCheckpoint is { } adopted)
                {
                    <span title="This node held no position for the blind node's log, and took the point where the log starts as its own.">Adopted checkpoint @adopted</span>
                }
            </div>
            @* What the owner of a copy or of a server blind node did (release 2.7): its limits told it to wait (it is not lost), or it was removed from the device (this PC revokes). *@
            @if (node.Contact?.PausedText is { Length: > 0 } pausedText)
            {
                <sl-alert variant="neutral" open style="margin-top:8px;" class="blind-paused">
                    <sl-icon slot="icon" name="pause-circle"></sl-icon>
                    @pausedText. Nothing is wrong with it: its owner's limit holds it, and it goes on by itself as soon as the limit allows.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @if (node.Contact?.LeftAt is { } leftAt)
            {
                <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-top:8px;" class="blind-left">
                    <sl-icon slot="icon" name="box-arrow-right"></sl-icon>
                    The owner of this device removed the copy from the device (@leftAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("g")). It stays in your network until you revoke it.
                    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Disconnect" data-busy="short" style="margin-top:8px;">
                        <input type="hidden" name="nodeId" value="@node.NodeId" />
                        <sl-button type="submit" size="small" variant="danger"><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="slash-circle"></sl-icon> Revoke</sl-button>
                    </form>
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @* The banners are the alarms the Api judged for this node (BlindAlarmService): the same list the desktop app notifies from. *@
            @if (node.Alarms.Contains("old_protocol"))
            {
                <sl-alert variant="danger" open style="margin-top:8px;">
                    This blind node runs an older sync protocol. Update it: until then no changes are delivered to it.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @if (node.Alarms.Contains("pc_too_old"))
            {
                <sl-alert variant="danger" open style="margin-top:8px;">
                    This blind node runs a newer sync protocol than this PC. Update this PC: until then it takes nothing from the blind node.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @if (node.Alarms.Contains("not_answering") && node.Contact is { NotAnsweringSince: { } notAnsweringSince })
            {
                <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-top:8px;">
                    This PC's calls to this blind node have failed since @notAnsweringSince.ToLocalTime().ToString("g")@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(node.Contact.NotAnsweringReason) ? "" : ": " + node.Contact.NotAnsweringReason).
                    Nothing is delivered to it meanwhile.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @if (node.Alarms.Contains("stale_baseline"))
            {
                <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-top:8px;">
                    This blind node's restore point is more than a week old. A restore from it would replay everything since. A new one is made
                    by a computer that holds the vault key; use Reseed once one of them has been running for a while.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            @if (node.Alarms.Contains("silent"))
            {
                <sl-alert variant="warning" open style="margin-top:8px;">
                    No contact for several days. While it is silent, other devices keep their history for it.
                    If it is gone for good, disconnect it.
                </sl-alert>
            }
            <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:10px;">
                <form data-busy="long" data-busy-title="Reseeding the blind node" method="post" asp-page-handler="Reseed">
                    <input type="hidden" name="nodeId" value="@node.NodeId" />
                    <sl-button type="submit" size="small"><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="arrow-repeat"></sl-icon> Reseed</sl-button>
                </form>
                <form data-busy="short" method="post" asp-page-handler="Disconnect">
                    <input type="hidden" name="nodeId" value="@node.NodeId" />
                    <sl-button type="submit" size="small" variant="danger"><sl-icon slot="prefix" name="x-circle"></sl-icon> Disconnect</sl-button>
                </form>
            </div>
        </div>
    }
}
